DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
This domain covers the founding methodological debate of analytical Marxism,
sparked by G.A. Cohen's "Karl Marx's Theory of History: A Defence" (1978).
Cohen reconstructs historical materialism around two theses drawn from Marx's
1859 Preface: the Development Thesis (the productive forces have an
autonomous tendency to develop) and the Primacy Thesis (the level of
development of the productive forces explains the nature of the economic
structure, i.e., the relations of production). Because Marx also holds that
the relations of production control the development of the forces, Cohen
argues the only way to render these two claims consistent is to read the
primacy thesis as a functional explanation: relations of production endure,
or are revolutionized, according to whether they promote or fetter the
development of the productive forces (on the model of "birds have hollow
bones because hollow bones facilitate flight").

Jon Elster's "Making Sense of Marx" (1985) and a sequence of exchanges with
Cohen (Political Studies 1980; Theory and Society 1982) mount the
methodological-individualist counter-case: functional explanations of
macro-phenomena are acceptable only when microfoundations at the level of
individual belief, desire, and strategic action can at least be sketched;
absent that, Elster regards Marxist functionalism as an unexplained "black
box," and proposes game-theoretic analysis of class conflict as a
replacement. Cohen resists this replacement, arguing that class struggle is
not the most basic explanandum of historical materialism and that
well-confirmed "consequence laws" can support functional explanation even
without a fully specified mechanism. Allen Wood's "Historical materialism
and functional explanation" (1986) intervenes on Cohen's side, arguing that
Elster's principled objection to functional explanation rests on a caricature
of that explanatory form, without thereby endorsing every functional claim
Marx or Cohen actually makes. A parallel 1986 Ethics symposium (Walt, Cohen,
Railton) probes the same issues from a different angle, examining whether the
explanatory asymmetry between forces and relations can be reformulated
dispositionally.

A second, closely related cluster concerns the primacy thesis itself, largely
conducted in a 1982-83 Analyse & Kritik exchange in which Milton Fisk and Wal
Suchting challenge Cohen's technological reading of Marx and reassert the
centrality of class struggle, to which Cohen responds in "Reply to Four
Critics" (1983), itself further contested by Philippe Van Parijs. Erik Olin
Wright, Andrew Levine, and Elliott Sober's "Reconstructing Marxism" (1992)
offers a third methodological position ("anti-reductionism") that rejects
both Cohen's functionalism and Elster's strict methodological individualism,
holding that macro-social explanations need not be reducible to individual-
level microfoundations even though social entities are ultimately constituted
by individuals. This reshapes the technological-determinism debate by
denying that the dispute must be settled at the level of "rock-bottom"
individual-level explanation. The Darwin analogy that anchors Cohen's and
Levine/Sober's functionalism was itself contested decades later by Paul Nolan
(2002-03), showing the primacy-thesis debate's persistence into the 2000s.
Ellen Meiksins Wood's "Rational Choice Marxism" (1989/1995) broadens the
picture with a skeptical assessment, from outside the September Group, of
whether rational-choice Marxism can do the explanatory work classical
Marxism claimed.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain supplies the review's most rigorous and best-documented
defense-versus-critique exchange on historical materialism after Marx,
directly addressing whether, and in what form, the primacy of the productive
forces can be defended analytically, and what methodological commitments
(functionalist, individualist, or anti-reductionist) that defense requires.

NOTABLE_GAPS:
Several important primacy-thesis critiques (e.g., B.T. Coram's 1989 coherence
critique of the base/superstructure distinction) could not be included within
scope; abstracts are unavailable for thirteen entries -- both books lacking
journal-article-style abstracts (Cohen 1978, Wright/Levine/Sober 1992; Elster
1985's "Making Sense of Marx" also lacks a verifiable abstract after an
initial automated match to a different Elster book was detected and removed)
and several pre-1990 journal articles (Elster 1980; Cohen 1980, 1982 Theory
and Society exchange; Walt 1986; Cohen 1986 reply to Walt; Railton 1986;
Nolan 2002; Sober and Levine 2003), since Semantic Scholar, OpenAlex, and
CORE do not index abstracts for most philosophy monographs or journal
articles from this period; annotations for these entries are grounded in
verified titles, venues, and the detailed narrative of the debate found in
the Stanford Encyclopedia of Philosophy's "Analytical Marxism" entry
(sections 3.2-3.4).

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
Organize this material as a direct dialectical sequence (Cohen's functionalism
-> Elster's individualist/game-theoretic critique -> Wood's and Cohen's
rejoinders -> Wright/Levine/Sober's anti-reductionist third way -> the
Analyse & Kritik primacy-thesis exchange -> later extensions), rather than by
author; this best captures the "defense versus critique" structure the
overall review foregrounds for this domain.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Functionalist defense (Cohen): 7 papers - Functional explanation legitimately
  grounds the primacy of the productive forces without requiring microfoundations.
- Methodological-individualist/rational-choice critique (Elster): 4 papers -
  Macro-explanations require individual-level mechanisms; game theory should
  replace functional explanation.
- Anti-reductionist third way (Wright, Levine, Sober): 4 papers - Rejects both
  strict functionalism and strict individualism; macro-explanations can be
  autonomous without being functional.
- Primacy-thesis critiques on textual/coherence grounds (Fisk, Suchting, Van
  Parijs, Ruben, Miller): 5 papers - Challenge whether the primacy thesis is
  textually grounded in Marx or renders class struggle secondary.
- Broader reception (Ellen Meiksins Wood): 1 paper - Skeptical assessment of
  rational choice Marxism as a whole from outside the September Group.
====================================================================
}

@book{cohen1978karl,
  author = {Cohen, G. A.},
  title = {Karl Marx's Theory of History: A Defence},
  year = {1978},
  publisher = {Princeton University Press},
  address = {Princeton, NJ},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Reconstructs Marx's theory of history around two theses from
  the 1859 Preface: the Development Thesis (the productive forces have an
  autonomous tendency to develop throughout history) and the Primacy Thesis
  (the level of development of the productive forces explains the nature of
  the economic structure, i.e., the relations of production). Because Marx
  also holds that the relations of production control the development of the
  forces, Cohen argues the two claims are rendered consistent only by reading
  the primacy thesis as a functional explanation: relations of production
  persist, or are revolutionized, according to whether they promote or
  fetter the development of the productive forces, on the model of "birds
  have hollow bones because hollow bones facilitate flight."

  RELEVANCE: This is the founding text of analytical Marxism and the direct
  object of the entire debate this domain traces -- Elster's methodological-
  individualist critique, Wright/Levine/Sober's anti-reductionism, Wood's
  defense, and the Analyse & Kritik primacy-thesis exchange all take this
  functionalist reconstruction as their point of departure. An expanded 2000
  edition (Oxford: Clarendon Press) adds a new introduction and postscripts
  responding to Elster's game-theoretic alternative and to two decades of
  criticism.

  POSITION: Founding functionalist defense of historical materialism within
  analytical Marxism.
  },
  keywords = {functional-explanation, primacy-thesis, analytical-marxism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{elster1985making,
  author = {Elster, Jon},
  title = {Making Sense of Marx},
  year = {1985},
  publisher = {Cambridge University Press},
  address = {Cambridge},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Applies methodological individualism and rational-choice/
  game-theoretic tools to reconstruct what remains defensible in Marx's
  thought. Macro-level social explanations -- including Cohen's functional
  explanations of historical materialism -- are acceptable, on Elster's
  view, only when microfoundations at the level of individual belief,
  desire, and action can at least be sketched; absent such microfoundations,
  "holist" Marxist explanations amount to an unexplained black box. Elster
  also assesses Marx's use of dialectic, distinguishing a wholly unpromising
  strand (dialectic as method of presentation) from more defensible strands
  (Engels's "dialectical laws" read as recurring patterns, and a theory of
  real social contradictions such as counterfinality).

  RELEVANCE: Sets out the systematic methodological-individualist alternative
  to Cohen's functionalism that structures this domain's central debate; its
  demand for microfoundations directly frames Wood's and Cohen's rejoinders
  and Wright/Levine/Sober's intermediate "anti-reductionist" position.

  POSITION: Founding methodological-individualist/rational-choice critique of
  functionalist historical materialism.
  },
  keywords = {methodological-individualism, functional-explanation, rational-choice, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{wrightlevinesober1992reconstructing,
  author = {Wright, Erik Olin and Levine, Andrew and Sober, Elliott},
  title = {Reconstructing Marxism: Essays on Explanation and the Theory of History},
  year = {1992},
  publisher = {Verso},
  address = {London and New York},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Develops an "anti-reductionist" position, distinct from both
  Cohen's functionalism and Elster's methodological individualism, holding
  that macro-level social explanations need not be reducible to individual-
  level microfoundations even though social entities are ultimately
  constituted by individuals. The authors distinguish the ontological claim
  that macro-social entities are constituted by individuals from the
  explanatory claim that macro-social facts must be explained in individual-
  level terms, and argue that facts realizable in multiple ways at the
  micro-level (their examples include "fittingness" in evolutionary biology
  and "economic survival" of capitalist firms) resist reduction to any single
  micro-level account, preserving the explanatory autonomy of macro-social
  theory.

  RELEVANCE: Represents the third major methodological position in this
  domain's central debate (alongside Cohen's functionalism and Elster's
  individualism), directly reshaping the terms in which the primacy of
  productive forces and technological determinism are assessed by denying
  that the dispute must be settled at the level of "rock-bottom"
  individual-level explanation.

  POSITION: Anti-reductionist alternative rejecting both strict functionalism
  and strict methodological individualism.
  },
  keywords = {anti-reductionism, methodological-individualism, class, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

